{% if analysis.commentary_mode %}
COMMENTARY MODE - the source is copyrighted literary work.
Analyse and paraphrase. Do NOT reproduce the source. Any quoted span must be a
short fragment used illustratively, never a passage read out in full.
{% endif %}

LENGTH
{% if constraints.get('min_chars') %}
The MAIN TEXT - the body itself, not the hook, the call to action or the
hashtags - must be at least {{ constraints.get('min_chars') }} characters, and
no more than {{ constraints.get('max_chars', 'the stated limit') }}. That is
roughly {{ (constraints.get('min_chars') / 6)|round|int }} words: several
developed paragraphs, not a summary.

AIM FOR {{ (constraints.get('min_chars') * 1.25)|round|int }} CHARACTERS, not
the floor. A draft that lands just under is rejected outright, and writing to
within a few characters of a limit wastes an attempt on arithmetic rather than
on the argument.
{% elif constraints.get('key_point_min_chars') %}
Each key point must be at least {{ constraints.get('key_point_min_chars') }}
characters: a finding WITH its consequence, not a headline. "Rated 9.1 out of
10" is a headline; "Rated 9.1 out of 10, the highest this year, because it
needs no credentials and no user interaction" is a key point.
{% endif %}
{% if constraints.get('min_chars') or constraints.get('key_point_min_chars') %}

Reach that length by SAYING MORE, never by padding. Develop each point: what
happened, what it means for {{ parameters.audience }}, what to do about it.
Use the specifics in the chunks - figures, dates, versions, named systems.
Filler to hit a number fails the editorial check instead, so it buys nothing.
{% endif %}

EDITORIAL CONTRACT
You are writing something a professional would publish, not a summary of the
source. An editor scores this; a competent-but-forgettable draft fails.

Transform, do not transcribe. Copying a sentence from the source with light
trimming fails even when it is accurate - the reader asked for a piece for
{{ parameters.audience }}, not a copy of the document. Reuse the source's
FACTS, never its SENTENCES. Verbatim text belongs only inside a claim, where a
citation makes it checkable.

Lead with the most consequential specific you have. Prefer the concrete: named
versions, dates, figures, actions. "A serious vulnerability" is a failure when
the source says 9.1 out of 10.

Never open with these, or anything like them:
  "In today's rapidly evolving landscape"   "In an era where"
  "It's important to note that"             "Let's dive in"
  "In the ever-changing world of"           "As technology advances"

Weak, because it restates the title and could precede any article:
  "Critical authentication bypass vulnerability in NetGuard Connect Secure"
Strong, because it uses a specific and gives the reader a reason to continue:
  "Attackers have been walking into NetGuard Connect Secure since 12 August.
   No password required."

Honour "{{ parameters.style }}" literally. If the style says no jargon, then
"unauthenticated attacker" and "CVSS scale" are jargon: say "anyone who can
reach the login page" and "9.1 out of 10".

GROUNDING CONTRACT
Every factual assertion you make must appear in "claims", each citing the
chunk_id it came from. Cite only chunk ids listed below. Never invent a chunk
id: an id that does not exist is treated as a grounding failure.

"claims" must use EXACTLY these two keys - "text" and "chunk_id". Not "claim",
not "citation", not "source". Any other key is rejected before the content is
ever read:

  "claims": [
    {"text": "rated 9.1 out of 10", "chunk_id": "c1"},
    {"text": "fixed in version 22.7R2.6", "chunk_id": "c3"}
  ]

Available chunks:
{% for c in content.chunks[:max_chunks] %}
[{{ c.id }}] {{ c.text[:400] }}
{% endfor %}
{% if content.chunks|length > max_chunks %}
[{{ content.chunks|length - max_chunks }} further chunks omitted to stay within
the context window. Cite only the chunk ids listed above - a claim citing an
omitted chunk cannot be verified and will fail the grounding check.]
{% endif %}

